+# Review: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockShop.server.luau`
+
+Findings are ordered by how much they matter. The first four can be exploited
+by a client or lose player data, so fix them before building on the script.
+Line numbers refer to the file as it is now.
+
+Nothing here was run in Studio. The NaN cases in finding 2 come from reading
+the code, not from testing.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Buying can duplicate stock and spam the DataStore (critical)
+
+**Where:** `getCoins` / `setCoins` (lines 42-48) and the `BuyBlocks` handler
+(lines 89-94).
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- **Race condition.** `getCoins` calls `GetAsync`, which yields. If a client
+  fires `BuyBlocks` ten times in the same frame, all ten handlers read the same
+  balance before any of them writes. Each one passes the `coins < price` check
+  and adds stock, and the last `SetAsync` wins. The player gets ten purchases
+  and pays for one.
+- **A DataStore round trip on every purchase.** Each buy costs two DataStore
+  requests. A client spamming the remote uses up the server's request budget,
+  and then saves for every player on that server get throttled.
+- **No `pcall`.** `GetAsync` and `SetAsync` throw when DataStores are throttled
+  or down. Right now that error just kills the handler. If it is ever moved
+  after the stock grant, the player would get blocks without paying.
+
+**What to do instead:** keep each player's coins in memory for the session:
+
+- Load the balance once in `PlayerAdded`, with `pcall` and a retry. Don't let
+  the player buy until it has loaded.
+- Save it on `PlayerRemoving`, in `game:BindToClose`, and on an autosave
+  interval. Use `UpdateAsync`, not `SetAsync`, so a save never overwrites a
+  newer value from another server.
+- The buy handler then reads and writes the in-memory number with no yield,
+  so purchases can't interleave.
+
+Name anything that still yields with `Async` (`loadCoinsAsync`) so the yield
+is visible where it is called.
+
+## 2. Remote arguments are trusted (critical)
+
+**Where:** all three handlers: `BuyBlocks` (line 84), `PlaceBlock` (line 105),
+and `RemoveBlock` (line 127).
+
+**What's wrong:** the type annotations (`bt: string`, `amt: number`,
+`pos: Vector3`, `part: Instance`) only tell the type checker to trust the
+client. Nothing checks the values at runtime:
+
+- **`amt` = NaN** passes `amt < 1 or amt > MAX_PURCHASE`, because every
+  comparison with NaN is false. `price` becomes NaN, `coins < price` is false,
+  and `coins - price` (NaN) goes to `SetAsync`. If the DataStore accepts that
+  value, `playerStock[bt]` also becomes NaN. Then `(playerStock[bt] or 0) < 1`
+  on line 107 is always false, and the player can place unlimited blocks.
+- **`amt` = 1.5** is accepted, which gives fractional coin balances and stock.
+- **`bt`** that isn't a block type, or isn't a string, makes `PRICES[bt] * amt`
+  throw (arithmetic on nil). That's harmless, but it fills the server's error
+  output.
+- **`pos` with NaN components** makes `PlacementRules.snap` return a NaN
+  center. Every check in `PlacementRules.check` compares with `<` or `>`, so
+  they all pass, and the server creates a Part at a NaN position. If `pos`
+  isn't a Vector3, `snap` throws.
+- **`part`** = nil makes `part.Parent` throw on line 128.
+
+**What to do instead:** type every argument after `plr` as `unknown` and
+narrow it before using it, returning early when a check fails:
+
+```lua
+buyRemote.OnServerEvent:Connect(function(player: Player, blockType: unknown, amount: unknown)
+	if not BlockTypes.isBlockType(blockType) then
+		return
+	end
+	if
+		typeof(amount) ~= "number"
+		or amount ~= math.floor(amount) -- also rejects NaN
+		or amount < 1
+		or amount > MAX_PURCHASE
+	then
+		return
+	end
+	...
+end)
+```
+
+For `pos`, check `typeof(pos) == "Vector3"` and that every component is
+finite (`x == x` and `math.abs(x) ~= math.huge`). Putting the finite check in
+`PlacementRules.check` protects the client preview too. For `part`, check
+`typeof(part) == "Instance"` before reading `.Parent`.
+
+`BlockTypes.isBlockType` returns `boolean`, so the checker won't narrow
+`blockType` from it. Cast after the check (`blockType :: BlockTypes.BlockType`),
+or change it to a type guard if luau-lsp supports one for your setup.
+
+## 3. Bought blocks are lost when the player leaves (high)
+
+**Where:** `stock` (line 40) and `PlayerRemoving` (lines 146-148).
+
+**What's wrong:** coins are saved to a DataStore, but stock only lives in
+memory and is cleared on leave. A player who buys 50 Brick and disconnects
+has paid for them and loses them.
+
+**What to do instead:** decide whether stock should persist.
+
+- If it should, save it next to coins, ideally as one record per player
+  (`{ Coins = 120, Stock = { Brick = 3 } }`) so the two can't get out of sync.
+- If it shouldn't, refund unplaced stock on leave, or sell blocks at the
+  moment they're placed so there is no stock to lose.
+
+## 4. Removing blocks is broken for orphans and fragile for owners (high)
+
+**Where:** `getOwnerName` (lines 50-57) and the `RemoveBlock` handler (lines
+132-139).
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- **The orphan path always errors.** When the owner has left, `getOwnerName`
+  runs off the end of the function and returns *no values*, not `nil`.
+  `tostring()` with no argument throws "missing argument #1". So the feature
+  in the comment on line 132 ("anyone can clear away a block whose owner has
+  left") has never worked: those requests error.
+- **It compares names, not IDs.** Converting to a string and comparing with
+  `"nil"` means a player whose username is literally `nil` would have their
+  blocks treated as ownerless. The block already stores `OwnerId`, so there
+  is no reason to go through names.
+- **Once it's fixed, removing an orphan refunds the remover.** Line 139 gives
+  the person who removed the block a free block of that type. A player could
+  farm stock from every block a departed player left behind.
+- **Orphans are never cleaned up.** Placed blocks stay in `PlacedBlocks` for
+  the whole life of the server.
+
+**What to do instead:** compare user IDs directly, and only refund the owner:
+
+```lua
+local ownerId = part:GetAttribute("OwnerId")
+local isOwner = ownerId == player.UserId
+local isOwnerPresent = typeof(ownerId) == "number"
+	and Players:GetPlayerByUserId(ownerId) ~= nil
+if not isOwner and isOwnerPresent then
+	return
+end
+
+part:Destroy()
+if isOwner then
+	-- refund
+end
+```
+
+`getOwnerName` can then be deleted. Also decide what happens to a player's blocks when they
+leave: destroy them in `PlayerRemoving`, or keep them and accept the cleanup
+rule above.
+
+## 5. Receipts can never be turned off (medium)
+
+**Where:** line 99.
+
+**What's wrong:** `plr:GetAttribute("ShowReceipts") or DEFAULT_SHOW_RECEIPTS`
+replaces `false` with `true`, because `or` treats `false` the same as `nil`.
+A player who sets `ShowReceipts = false` still gets receipts.
+
+**What to do instead:** use the same `== nil` check you already use for
+`AutoAnchor` on lines 118-121:
+
+```lua
+local showReceipts = player:GetAttribute("ShowReceipts")
+if showReceipts == nil then
+	showReceipts = DEFAULT_SHOW_RECEIPTS
+end
+```
+
+## 6. `stock[plr]` can be nil inside the handlers (medium)
+
+**Where:** `stock` setup (lines 142-148), and the reads on lines 96, 106,
+and 139.
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- Players who joined before `PlayerAdded` was connected never get an entry.
+  This happens regularly in Studio playtests, and also whenever the script
+  starts late. Every handler then throws when it indexes `nil`.
+- The buy handler yields on `GetAsync`, so a player can leave while it's
+  waiting. When it resumes, `stock[plr]` is nil and line 97 throws *after*
+  coins have already been deducted.
+
+The type `{ [Player]: { [string]: number } }` hides both problems, because
+Luau types a lookup of a missing key as the value type, not as nil.
+
+**What to do instead:**
+
+- Run the join handler for players already in the game:
+  `for _, player in Players:GetPlayers() do onPlayerAdded(player) end`
+- Type the map as `{ [Player]: { [BlockTypes.BlockType]: number? }? }` so the
+  checker makes every handler deal with the missing entry.
+- Guard with `if not playerStock then return end`.
+
+Fixing finding 1 removes the yield from the buy handler, which also takes
+care of the mid-purchase case.
+
+## 7. Unanchored blocks can be moved by clients (medium)
+
+**Where:** lines 118-124 and `placeBlock` line 71.
+
+**What's wrong:** when `AutoAnchor` is false, the block is an unanchored Part.
+Roblox automatically hands physics ownership of it to a nearby client, and
+that client can then move it anywhere. That gets around `PlacementRules`
+(reach, height, overlap). Unanchored blocks also fall and tumble, so the grid
+stops matching what's actually in the world.
+
+**What to do instead:** if unanchored blocks are a real feature, call
+`part:SetNetworkOwner(nil)` after parenting, so the server owns the physics.
+If the setting isn't needed yet, drop it and always anchor.
+
+## 8. No rate limit, and every rejected placement logs a warning (medium-low)
+
+**Where:** line 114 (`warn`), line 79 (`print`), and all three handlers.
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- Nothing limits how often a client can fire the remotes. Each placement runs
+  a spatial query (`GetPartBoundsInBox`).
+- Each rejected placement writes a `warn`, so a spamming client can flood the
+  server output and bury real errors.
+- Rejections are also normal gameplay (the client preview and the server can
+  disagree by a frame), so they aren't worth a warning.
+- The `print` on every placement adds noise in a live server.
+
+**What to do instead:**
+
+- Add a simple per-player cooldown or token bucket on each remote.
+- Remove the `warn`, or send the rejection reason back to the client so the
+  UI can show it.
+- Drop the placement `print`, which also removes the `log` parameter (see
+  finding 10).
+
+## 9. Types: missing `--!strict` and loose key types (low, but finds bugs)
+
+**Where:** line 1, line 14, line 40, and line 50.
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- The file has no `--!strict`, while both sibling modules in `Shared` use it.
+  Without it, several of the problems above don't show up in the editor.
+- `PRICES` is keyed by `string` and repeats the block list from
+  `BlockTypes`. Adding a sixth block type means editing both lists, and
+  nothing warns you if you forget one.
+- `stock` is typed so that missing keys look present (see finding 6).
+- `getOwnerName` has no return type and doesn't return a value on every path
+  (see finding 4).
+
+**What to do instead:**
+
+- Add `--!strict` as line 1.
+- Type `PRICES` as `{ [BlockTypes.BlockType]: number }`, or move each price
+  into `BlockTypes.Styles` (or a sibling `BlockPrices` module) so each block
+  is defined in one place.
+- Use `number?` values in maps that are read with keys that may be missing.
+
+## 10. `placeBlock` takes five arguments, including two booleans (low)
+
+**Where:** lines 59-82, called on line 124 as
+`placeBlock(plr, center, bt, autoAnchor, true)`.
+
+**What's wrong:** a bare `true` at the call site doesn't say what it does.
+`log` is always `true`, so it isn't really a parameter. The return value
+is never used.
+
+**What to do instead:** remove `log` (see finding 8), and move `anchored`
+into an options table:
+`placeBlock(player, center, blockType, { Anchored = autoAnchor })`.
+
+## 11. Naming (low)
+
+**Where:** throughout.
+
+**What's wrong and what to do instead:**
+
+- Write abbreviations out in full: `plr` -> `player`, `amt` -> `amount`,
+  `bt` -> `blockType`, `pos` -> `position`, and `p` -> `player` on line 52.
+- Functions that yield end in `Async`: `getCoins` -> `getCoinsAsync`, and the
+  same for `setCoins` (or replace them, per finding 1).
+- Name the handlers and connect them by name (`onBuyBlocks`, `onPlaceBlock`,
+  `onRemoveBlock`, `onPlayerAdded`, `onPlayerRemoving`). This makes the file
+  easier to scan, and finding 6 needs `onPlayerAdded` named anyway so it can
+  be called for players who are already in the game.
+
+## 12. Remotes are created at runtime (low)
+
+**Where:** `makeRemote` (lines 24-34).
+
+**What's wrong:** the remotes only exist after this script runs, so every
+client script has to `WaitForChild` each one. They also go straight into the
+root of `ReplicatedStorage`, which gets crowded as more systems add their own.
+
+**What to do instead:** define the remotes in the place, either through
+`default.project.json` or a `.model.json` under
+`src/ReplicatedStorage/Remotes`. Then the server and the client can both look
+them up from a single `Remotes` folder.
+
+---
+
+## Before building more on it
+
+This one script currently handles coins (persistence), inventory (stock),
+placement, and removal. The fixes above add session loading, saving, and
+rate limiting, and those make the file harder to change if they all live in
+one place. I'd split it before adding features:
+
+- A coins/profile module that owns loading, the in-memory balance, and saving.
+- An inventory module that owns stock.
+- This script, kept as the remote layer: it validates arguments and calls the
+  other two.
+
+The README lists Module Loader as a package. If you plan to use it, this
+split is a good time to move to that structure.
